A||||8||Fformula88||Who has installed a sunroof.||||1||
Z||000000||Fformula88||10-25-2000||05:28 PM||||I was wondering who has installed a Sunroof in their non-sr equipped Fiero.  I am thinking of getting one this spring and was wondering what the best way to do it is.  Basically, I was wondering if its easier to get the roof panel out of another Fiero that had a factory Sunroof and swap it out with mine, or just install a new one.  I know where I can get one out of an 84 Fiero at a reasonable price.  Is it worth it or should I go new?  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">  <BR>1988 Fiero Formula||||reg||1
Z||000001||TheFiero64||10-25-2000||05:39 PM||||I have. I got the factory one off of my parts car. You must take your time when pulling the roof off so you don't break the A pillars. There is a lot of adhesive around the windshield. I would only go for a factory unit. It is the best looking and is a nice big size. Also it stores over the spare tire. Make sure you get the parts that hold it in wile it is stored and the wind deflector. I will take pics later and post them to show you where the bolts and glue are so you can have a trouble free job.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Robert||10-25-2000||07:06 PM||||Here is a good link for your sunroof install.<BR>I had mine done by George at the Fiero Shop . <BR> <A HREF="http://www.cyberverse.com/~cyco/fieroparts/tips/sunroof.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cyberverse.com/~cyco/fieroparts/tips/sunroof.htm</A> <P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://canterburytrails.com/photo/T2.GIF"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-25-2000||08:06 PM||||I've looked at allot of aftermarket roofs. There are very few good ones that don't look like an afterthought.<P>Haven't converted one, already have it, but I have looked at them in the scrap yard.<P>They aren't hard to get off. Just real time consuming to deal with the goop along the windshield.<P>Most people have been using music wire to seperate the goop but there's a tool used for seperating windshields and other glued glass from the car that might make it easier. The tool has an L shaped blade on the end of a handle. There is a second handle tied by a small cable or rod to the cutting end of the first handle. You use the second handle to pull the blade while you guide it with the main handle. <P>These people might have it.... <A HREF="http://www.toolsusa.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.toolsusa.com/</A>  They are one of the places you can get the door hinge spring tool. I haven't looked there. last time I saw one of these was at the local farmers market tool shop. was less than $20.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-25-2000||08:10 PM||||They do have that goop cutter tool. <A HREF="http://www.toolsusa.com/ToolsUSA/itempg.icl?itmid=16326&itmcnt=162&numitems=163&secid=9&eflag=0&orderidentifier=ID97251808532851387C46145170" TARGET=_blank>winshield tool</A> <P>I bet a custom made or modified blade on this would make getting the rear glass out a hell of allot easier too.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||10-25-2000||09:42 PM||||I have done the swap. You cannot use the windsheild "L" tool it is not long enough. The goop across the front is 2" back. The wire would work but we used a piece of sheet metal with a wooden handle 18" long X 2" wide and strips if cardboard. You cut along with the "blade" and place cardboard where you had cut. The goop will stick to itself after you cut it. It took about 2-3 hours to do the swap and the windsheild does not need to come out. You do need to remove the plastic/rubber bead that is between the windshield and roof and it does need to be glued back in. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||wkayl||10-26-2000||10:16 AM||||I removed one to replace the after market sunroof in my car.  Here are some pictures of the removal:<P>Cutting the goop from around the bottom of the windshield with piano wire.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/front_window_4.jpg"> <P>Cutting the side.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side_wind_2.jpg"> <P>Cutting the top side of the windshield (notice cardboard keeping it from becoming restuck.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/top_one.jpg"> <P>I used a screwdriver handle to hold the top up.  Be sure to remove the nuts on the bolts behind each front corner of the top, not only the ones around the sunroof opening.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/top_three.jpg"> <P>And its off!!!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/top_off.jpg"> <P>I covered it and hauled it home in my camper to keep it out of the wind.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/top_shipping.jpg"> <P>I still have to repaint it (red) and install it this winter.<P><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000007||Oreif||10-26-2000||11:00 AM||||Just to let you know I think different years had different amout of goop. The 86 we did the first swap on only had goop across the top. The 84 we removed the roof off of had goop all the way around like the pics from wkayl shows. Has anyone else who strips cars noticed a difference?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tigger||10-26-2000||11:29 AM||||My '84 had goop across the top and down the pillars.<P>That's interesting. I wonder if the guy in charge of putting the goop on in the Fiero plant, decided to put the goop only across the top that day.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Anyone else do a swap on different year Fieros?<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif"> ||||reg||1
